### Java Enumerations (`enum`)

**Definition:**
An **`enum`** in Java is a specialized class type whose fields consist of a fixed set of predefined constants.

#### Key Characteristics:
- Extends `java.lang.Enum` implicitly (cannot extend other classes).
- Type-safe: Prevents passing invalid arbitrary integer constants.
- Enums can have **constructors, instance variables, and methods**.
- Directly supported as switch selectors in `switch` statements.

```java
enum Day { MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY, FRIDAY, SATURDAY, SUNDAY }

Day today = Day.FRIDAY;
switch (today) {
    case FRIDAY -> System.out.println("Weekend ahead!");
    default -> System.out.println("Weekday");
}
```

$$\therefore \text{Enums replace error-prone integer constants with compile-time type safety.}$$
